Reading time badges look precise — "6 min read" — but they're built on a number nobody measured: how fast you, specifically, read. We use 230 words per minute, which is a commonly cited average, applied to a word count nobody agreed on either.

## The function is intentionally dumb

```ts
export function readingTime(source: string, wordsPerMinute = 230): number {
  const words = source.trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;
  return Math.max(1, Math.round(words / wordsPerMinute));
}
```

No adjustment for code blocks, images, or how dense the prose is. A post that's mostly a code sample gets the same per-word rate as one that's mostly narrative, even though nobody reads a code block at reading speed.

## Why we didn't make it smarter

We looked at weighting code blocks differently, and reading-level scoring, and gave up on both. The badge isn't a promise — it's a signal that lets a reader decide whether now is a good time to start. A number that's roughly right and always available beats one that's occasionally more accurate and requires guessing at someone's actual reading speed, which no formula can do anyway.

The floor of one minute matters more than any of that: a 40-word post rounding to "0 min read" looks like a bug, not an insight.
